  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14171842/assets/508818c87b85ec8720d681895f54c264c8252a9b5f98b8878e53b20ad336dca5.png)\n\nGiven that $M$ is the midpoint of $\\overset{―}{AB}$, what is the value of $x?$",
  "worked_solution": "Since $M$ is the midpoint of $\\overset{―}{AB}$, the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is twice the length of the segment $\\overset{―}{AM}.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2AM & =AB \\\\ 2(2x+1) & =5x-2 \\\\ 4x+2 & =5x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we subtract $4x$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+2-4x & =5x-2-4x \\\\ 2 & =x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we add $2$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+2 & =x-2+2 \\\\ 4 & =x \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$",
